  cycle of   -1: T-word 1            K =  1 steps, L = 1 odd, clock K/L = 1/1, 2^K - 3^L = -1
  cycle of   -5: T-word 110          K =  3 steps, L = 2 odd, clock K/L = 3/2, 2^K - 3^L = -1
  cycle of  -17: T-word 11110111000  K = 11 steps, L = 7 odd, clock K/L = 11/7, 2^K - 3^L = -139
  Stern-Brocot path to log2 3 (p/q = K/L): 1/1<, 2/1>, 3/2<, 5/3>, 8/5>, 11/7<, 19/12<, 27/17>, 46/29>, 65/41>, 84/53<, 149/94>, 233/147>, 317/200>
  the clocks 1/1, 3/2, 11/7 are SB-path nodes at depths 0, 2, 5, all LOWER
  approximations: a CHAIN on one path, not twins + container; 11/7 = mediant(3/2, 8/5), and 8/5 is an upper node
  cycle -1: cyclic word balanced (Christoffel/Sturmian) = True
  cycle -5: cyclic word balanced (Christoffel/Sturmian) = True
  cycle -17: cyclic word balanced (Christoffel/Sturmian) = False
  the balanced word of slope 7/11 is 01011011011 -- the -17 word 11110111000 is not a rotation of it:
  so '11/7 = 3/2 (+) 8/5' holds for the clocks only; no mediant structure on the cycle words (NUMEROLOGY)

====================================================================================================
T6  four-vertex tournaments
====================================================================================================
  one-arc-reversal adjacencies between classes: [('C3+sink', 'TT'), ('C3+sink', 'strong'), ('TT', 'source+C3'), ('TT', 'strong'), ('source+C3', 'strong')]
  converse: {'TT': 'TT', 'C3+sink': 'source+C3', 'source+C3': 'C3+sink', 'strong': 'strong'}   H: {'TT': [1], 'C3+sink': [3], 'strong': [5], 'source+C3': [3]}
  => KT-b with TWO self-dual members (TT, strong) around the converse pair; H(TT) + H(strong) = 1 + 5 =
     2 H(diamond): the diamonds sit at the arithmetic mean, like AM between GM and QM (a coincidence of
     small numbers: THM-4472 reads the pair as time reversal)

====================================================================================================
T7  Kohl colours and the places {inf, 2, 3}
====================================================================================================
  the colour of the doubling edge {m, 2m} is the mod-3 sign (m | 3) in (Z/3)^x = {+1, -1} (b = +1, c = -1);
  doubling flips it because 2 = -1 mod 3 (so doubling rays are properly 2-coloured), and nu flips it
  (nu b nu = c): the archimedean sign acts on the Tait colouring through the 3-adic sign.
  multiples of 3 have no mod-3 sign: they are exactly Kohl's deleted/leaf vertices 0(6), 3(6).

